1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,050
Welcome to Module Five.

2
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:04,700
Think about the last time you keyed out an insect.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,587
You did not read six hundred species accounts.

4
00:00:07,587 --> 00:00:09,712
You used a dichotomous key.

5
00:00:09,712 --> 00:00:11,525
A few short questions.

6
00:00:11,525 --> 00:00:14,200
Wings covering the abdomen, yes or no.

7
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:19,937
Each answer sent you to the next couplet, and within a minute you were on the one page that mattered.

8
00:00:19,937 --> 00:00:21,412
The key is small.

9
00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:23,025
The detail is deep.

10
00:00:23,025 --> 00:00:25,187
And you only open what you need.

11
00:00:25,187 --> 00:00:28,400
Today is about building that kind of key for your agent.

12
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,562
Every coding agent reads a special instruction file at the start of every session.

13
00:00:33,562 --> 00:00:44,800
Claude Code reads a file called Claude dot M D. Codex and many other tools read Agents dot M D. Whatever is in that file is loaded into the context window, every single time.

14
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,487
And after yesterday, you know that window is a crowded, expensive habitat.

15
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:51,000
So what goes in it?

16
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,925
The team at OpenAI that built a large product almost entirely with their coding agent tried the obvious approach first.

17
00:00:57,925 --> 00:01:00,675
One big instruction file with everything in it.

18
00:01:00,675 --> 00:01:02,512
It failed in four ways.

19
00:01:02,512 --> 00:01:04,625
It crowded out the actual work.

20
00:01:04,625 --> 00:01:07,862
When everything was marked important, nothing was.

21
00:01:07,862 --> 00:01:10,250
It went stale almost at once.

22
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:12,487
And nobody could check it automatically.

23
00:01:12,487 --> 00:01:14,025
Their fix was simple.

24
00:01:14,025 --> 00:01:19,687
Keep the instruction file to about a hundred lines, and treat it as a table of contents.

25
00:01:19,687 --> 00:01:25,550
The real knowledge lives in a folder of short documents, and the instruction file just says where to look.

26
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:27,950
They call this progressive disclosure.

27
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:32,000
Start small, and follow the pointers only when the task needs them.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,787
Claude Code's creator gives similar advice.

29
00:01:34,787 --> 00:01:36,400
Keep the file short.

30
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:41,650
Put in the commands, the important files, the conventions, and the things the agent must never do.

31
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:48,362
Files can also nest, so a folder can carry its own small map that loads only when the agent works there.

32
00:01:48,362 --> 00:01:50,700
Now, where does the metaphor fail?

33
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:55,987
A field key is written once, about organisms that do not change while you are keying them.

34
00:01:55,987 --> 00:02:00,787
Your codebase changes every day, sometimes because of the agent itself.

35
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:04,325
A wrong couplet in a field guide wastes a few minutes.

36
00:02:04,325 --> 00:02:08,375
A wrong line in your agent's map gets followed, confidently.

37
00:02:08,375 --> 00:02:16,162
That is why the OpenAI team checked their map automatically, and even ran an agent whose only job was to tidy stale documents.

38
00:02:16,162 --> 00:02:24,412
In today's lab you will create the repository for your capstone project, the small ecosystem of agents you will build by the end of this course.

39
00:02:24,412 --> 00:02:30,187
Your coding agent will interview you first, then draft a short map and a few focused documents.

40
00:02:30,187 --> 00:02:35,175
You will add a tiny script that fails if the map points at a file that no longer exists.

41
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:39,237
Then you will test the whole thing in a fresh session, and try to break it.

42
00:02:39,237 --> 00:02:41,987
Open your terminal, and let's draw the map.

